{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And they shall spread the cloth</b> — this is a metaphor; the matter is as clear as a cloth. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 22:17), and it is the midrash of Rabbi Yishmael in the Sifrei (Sifrei Tetzeh §237) and in the Mekhilta (Mishpatim §13). But there is no need for this, for formerly in Israel they would bring the groom and bride into the bridal chamber and examine them, and the witnesses would stand guard for them from outside — these are what the Sages call שׁוֹשְׁבִינִין (attendants) — and when they had separated, the witnesses would enter there and take the cloth (שִׂמְלָה) upon which he had lain with her, and see the blood. This is known from the Talmud (Ketubot 46a; Yerushalmi ad loc. ch. 1 hal. 1) and from books of Aggadah, and they call that cloth a סוּדָר. Therefore Scripture says that her father and mother shall spread the cloth that they took from the hand of the witnesses, and they shall say: these are the tokens of my daughter's virginity. And surely the matter requires explanation in many respects concerning which Scripture is brief; but he did not say 'this is a metaphor' except for the one who holds that one who spreads an evil report is liable even if he did not consummate, whereas the plain sense of Scripture follows the one who says, until he consummates. So too they said in the Gemara (Ketubot 46a): Rabbi Elazar ben Ya'akov says, an actual cloth — and the halakhah follows his words. Such is also the sense of the passage: Scripture first says, 'and the father of the girl and her mother shall take and bring out the tokens of the girl's virginity' — speaking of both of them; then it returns to the father alone and says, 'and the father of the girl...' and then says again, 'and they shall spread the cloth' — both of them. And the reason this claim belongs to the father alone is that the fine is his; but the mother is included in the act because the matter of the cloth is something women deal with, and they are the ones knowledgeable and expert in blood, and it is fitting for the mother to seize it and bring it to the court (בית דין). But the claim <b>'I gave my daughter to this man'</b> is the father's alone, and the mother has no standing in his case; rather, after the father sets forth his claims in court, he seizes the cloth that his wife brought under the corners of her garment, and they spread it out, the two of them, in court. Now the mother was not mentioned in the passage and does not come to court at all except on account of the matter of the cloth — which is literal, exactly as I have explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase מְחֻוָרִין הַדְבָרִים כְּשִׂמְלָה plays on the word שִׂמְלָה (cloth/garment) meaning both 'clear/evident' and 'like a cloth'; translated hyper-literally to preserve the wordplay."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:38.953512076Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}